  },
  {
    cite: 'Blume, B. D. et al. (2010). Transfer of training: a meta-analytic review. Journal of Management, 36(4).',
    href: 'https://doi.org/10.1177/0149206309352880',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Does this transfer?"
  description="What the research says about learning strategy through games, what Chaalwise does about it, and what it cannot promise."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Does this transfer?</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Partly, and only if it is taught for. Playing games on its own does little for general thinking. An idea is more likely to carry over to work and study
      when it is named, met in different settings and used on your own decisions.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="evidence-title">
      <h2 id="evidence-title">What the evidence says</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Getting good at a game makes you good at that game.</strong> Studies of chess lessons find small effects on school results and general
          thinking, shrinking to about nothing in the best-controlled studies (Sala and Gobet 2016, 2017). A trial of chess lessons in 100 English primary
          schools found no effect on maths, reading or science (Jerrim and others 2018). Transfer to a distant setting is rare, and depends on how far the
          settings differ (Barnett and Ceci 2002).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ideas move when they are bridged.</strong> Naming the idea and asking where else it applies makes transfer more likely (Perkins and Salomon
          1988). Comparing two cases of the same idea did far more than studying the same two cases one at a time (Gentner, Loewenstein and Thompson 2003).
          Explaining to yourself why an answer is right helps too (Bisra and others 2018).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Short training can change decisions.</strong> One session of game-based training with named biases and personal feedback reduced biased
          answers on new kinds of problems, and the effect lasted at least two months (Morewedge and others 2015). In a later study, trained students made fewer
          biased choices in a business case they did not know was a test (Sellier and others 2019).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Use at work depends on work.</strong> Training is used more when people are supported in trying it at work (Blume and others 2010). A plan of
          the form "When this happens, I will do that" helps people act (Gollwitzer and Sheeran 2006).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="does-title">
      <h2 id="does-title">What Chaalwise does about it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Each lesson names its idea, and says where it turns up at work and in study.</li>
        <li>Each idea is shown in a game and in at least three cases away from the board, so reviews bring the idea back in different cases.</li>
        <li>Strategy lessons set a board position beside a case from work or study, to compare.</li>
        <li>Each strategy lesson ends with a plan, "When …, I will …", and the review asks later whether you used it.</li>
        <li>Questions come back just before you would forget them.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="cannot-title">
      <h2 id="cannot-title">What it cannot promise</h2>
      <p>
        Chaalwise cannot see your work. It measures your answers to questions, not what you do on Monday. A strong level on your progress page means you know an
        idea well and can spot it in a new case, which is a start, not proof that you use it. Whether you do depends on using it soon, on real decisions, and on
        the people around you.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you run a team: give people a decision to use the idea on within a week, and ask how it went. <a href="/pack/">The printable pack</a> is made for
        that.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sources-title">
      <h2 id="sources-title">Sources</h2>
      <ol class="sources">
        {SOURCES.map((s) => (
          <li>
            {s.cite} <a href={s.href}>{s.href.replace('https://', '')}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
